    "q-103492": {
      "dom_id": "step-q103492",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Given that $15≡7\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$, which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\n- $n=2$\n- $n=4$\n- $n=6$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=15-7=8.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $2∣8$ because $8÷2=4,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=2.$\n\n- Note that $4∣8$ because $8÷4=2,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=4.$\n\n- Note that $6∤8.$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
